      "title": "[scotsman.com] European researchers test farm water systems as climate swings threaten crops",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://kagiproxy.com/img/9MYFWxXwF_loyWc6MZu49fTPr0E5AOs1My_PvL_t3EYCmEG6uiOfZ8bGljyJNpnX9LBPbRXCFQhvU25hXGGkycdSP3g1BQAkL6fBFpY8E3hzdkYsiif3J0dQ0Sc2fW-GQDxs87fA2jQbRaU\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\nScotland’s summer of 2026 brought earlier harvests, heat stress in spring-sown crops and rapidly falling water levels, while research indicates extreme day-to-night temperature swings have become more frequent since the 1980s. Those swings can disrupt crop growth. The Scotsman reported Scottish cereal yields below historic averages, with results varying by crop, soil and location.\n\nA Swedish research project is testing a retention-irrigation system that stores water during dry periods and releases it during floods to develop recommendations for its use. In Spain, agricultural cooperatives say farmers are working to adapt and cut emissions but warn that putting innovations into practice depends on farm profitability and adequate funding under the next EU farm-policy reform.",
